      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND/AIMS",
            "content": "Bronchial asthma continues to be a big challenge to therapy. Mast cells play an important role in allergic asthma. Histamine and leukotrienes are established mast cell mediators, but antihistamines currently play no role in asthma therapy."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Human bronchial strips were exposed to the mast cell activator compound 48/80 (200 μg/ml) in isolated organ experiments."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The contractile response was not inhibited by the H1 receptor antagonist antihistamine chloropyramine (0.3 μmol/l), the leukotriene cys-LT1 receptor antagonist MK 571 (3 μmol/l), the 5-lipoxygenase inhibitor MK 886 (5 μmol/l), the cyclo-oxygenase inhibitor indomethacin (5 μmol/l), tetrodotoxin, or atropine. Chloropyramine, combined with either MK 571 or MK 886 significantly reduced the response."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "A supra-additive effect is proposed for the antihistamine and the anti-leukotrienes, which might have relevance to human asthma therapy as well; such a combination deserves a large-scale clinical study. These data also indicate that substances like compound 48/80 should be denoted as mast cell activators rather than 'histamine liberators'."
          }
        ]
      },
